- Retrieved practices:
- Authentication error handling and timing-attack-safe password comparison
WHAT: Revealing whether a username exists via distinct error messages and using naive string comparison for passwords can leak valid accounts and enable timing attacks.
WHY: Attackers can enumerate registered users and infer correct passwords by measuring response-time differences, leading to account takeover.
HOW: Always return a generic authentication error (e.g., “Invalid credentials”) regardless of whether the username exists or the password is wrong; use constant-time comparison functions for password checks and invoke them even when the user record is missing (e.g., compare against an empty value).
EXAMPLE: Before: if (!user) return "User not found"; if (user.pw == inputPw) login(); After: fetchedHash = user ? user.pwHash : emptyHash; if (ConstantTimeCompare(fetchedHash, Hash(inputPw))) login(); else return "Invalid credentials".

- Secure handling of authentication data in web applications
WHAT: Authentication data is exposed if passwords are visible on screen, stored via “remember me”/autocomplete, sent over unencrypted HTTP, included in URLs/query strings, or if error messages reveal whether username or password is incorrect.
WHY: Leaked credentials and detailed error feedback enable credential theft, brute-force optimization, and user impersonation.
HOW: Use `<input type="password" autocomplete="off">`, disable “remember me”/browser autocomplete for credentials, send credentials only via HTTPS using HTTP POST (never in URLs/query strings), and return a single generic error message for all authentication failures.
EXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `<form method="get" action="http://example.com/login"><input name="password"></form>`
After (secure): `<form method="post" action="https://example.com/login" autocomplete="off"><input type="password" name="password" autocomplete="off"></form>`

- SQL Injection Prevention
WHAT: Building SQL queries by concatenating user-controlled input directly into the query string allows arbitrary SQL to be injected and executed by the database.
WHY: Attackers can bypass authorization, read or modify sensitive data (e.g., all credit cards), or destroy data, leading to full database compromise.
HOW: Never concatenate untrusted input into SQL; instead, use parameterized queries/prepared statements with placeholders and bind all external values as parameters, following the placeholder syntax of your specific database/driver.
EXAMPLE:
Before (unsafe): query := "SELECT number FROM creditcards WHERE customerId = " + customerId
After (safe):  query := "SELECT number FROM creditcards WHERE customerId = ?"; db.QueryContext(ctx, query, customerId)

- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
WHAT: Risk of SQL injection when building SQL queries by concatenating or interpolating user-controlled input instead of using parameterized queries or prepared statements.
WHY: Attackers can inject arbitrary SQL, leading to data theft, corruption, or full compromise of the database.
HOW: Always use prepared statements with bound parameters for any user-supplied values; only consider non-prepared queries if robust input validation and output encoding are implemented in a separate, enforced layer and the database truly cannot support prepared statements.
EXAMPLE (Go, before): db.Exec("UPDATE creditcards SET name='" + customerName + "' WHERE customerId=233")
EXAMPLE (Go, after): db.Exec("UPDATE creditcards SET name=? WHERE customerId=?", customerName, 233)

- Sensitive data exposure in comments and URLs
WHAT: Leaving sensitive endpoints or credentials in source-code comments, or passing secrets (API keys, tokens, session IDs, PINs) in HTTP GET query strings, exposes them via code, logs, browser history, proxies, and search engines.
WHY: Attackers can recover these values from source control, comments, logs, intercepted traffic, or cached URLs and then impersonate users, call internal APIs, or escalate privileges.
HOW: Never store real credentials or secret endpoints in comments; use configuration/secret management instead. Do not send sensitive data in URLs; send it in the request body or headers over HTTPS, and use short-lived, high-entropy tokens. Ensure logs and histories do not contain secrets.
EXAMPLE (Go, before): req, _ := http.NewRequest("GET", "http://mycompany.com/api/mytoken?api_key=000s3cr3t000", nil)
EXAMPLE (Go, after): req, _ := http.NewRequest("POST", "https://mycompany.com/api/mytoken", bytes.NewBuffer(body)); req.Header.Set("Authorization", "Bearer "+token)

- Use stored procedures to restrict direct data access
WHAT: Directly querying tables (e.g., `SELECT * FROM tblUsers WHERE userId = $user_input`) can expose sensitive columns (such as passport IDs) to application/database users who should not see them.
WHY: Overly broad access lets users or compromised accounts read confidential data beyond what their role requires, increasing data‑leak and privacy‑breach risk.
HOW: Define stored procedures that return only the necessary columns, grant applications/users EXECUTE permission on those procedures, and revoke/limit direct SELECT on underlying tables; combine this with proper input validation and least‑privilege DB roles.
EXAMPLE: Before: `SELECT * FROM tblUsers WHERE userId = @userId;` After (procedure): `CREATE PROCEDURE db.getName @userId INT AS SELECT name, lastname FROM tblUsers WHERE userId = @userId; GRANT EXECUTE ON db.getName TO app_user; REVOKE SELECT ON tblUsers FROM app_user;`



